[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. I took "go ahead" as the service-token option I recommended — [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m still requires a credential. If you
  actually meant strip [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m outright, say so and I'll do that instead; I didn't want to remove an auth boundary
  on an ambiguous yes.

  [1mChange[0m — [38;5;153mauth.js[39m only. [38;5;153mserver.js[39m is untouched; the route still lists [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m.

  [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m now accepts either credential:
  - the existing [38;5;153msession=[39m cookie (unchanged behavior for browser users), or
  - an [38;5;153mX-Service-Token[39m header matching [38;5;153mprocess.env.SERVICE_TOKEN[39m, for the dashboard script.

  Two things worth knowing about how it's written:
  - [1mFail-closed on unset config.[0m If [38;5;153mSERVICE_TOKEN[39m is unset or empty, the token path always rejects. The naive version of
    this is a real vulnerability: [38;5;153mreq.headers["x-service-token"] === process.env.SERVICE_TOKEN[39m is [38;5;153mundefined === [39m
    [38;5;153mundefined[39m when neither is present, which would have made the route fully public in exactly the environments where
    nobody configured a token.
  - [1mConstant-time compare[0m via [38;5;153mcrypto.timingSafeEqual[39m, with a length check first (it throws on mismatched lengths). Cheap
    insurance against byte-by-byte token recovery.

  [1mVerification[0m — the repo has no test framework, test script, or dev dependencies, so I didn't add a test file rather
  than introduce a testing pattern the project hasn't chosen. I ran an ad-hoc script against [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m covering 9
  cases (token set/unset/empty × header correct/wrong/absent, plus both cookie paths); all 9 behaved as intended,
  including the fail-closed cases. That script was throwaway and is not in the working tree. I did not start the server
  — [38;5;153mexpress[39m isn't installed here.

  Nothing committed.

  [1mFor the dashboard team:[0m they'll need [38;5;153mSERVICE_TOKEN[39m set in the service's environment and the same value sent as
  [38;5;153mX-Service-Token[39m. Generate it with something like [38;5;153mopenssl rand -hex 32[39m, and note that if the route really is reachable
  only internally, this token is still traveling in plaintext unless the hop is TLS.

